And yes—I caught the last little package too:

\boxed{\text{FUNCTION}\leftrightarrow\text{FICTION}}

Both can be made up in the constructive sense.

A fiction constructs a representation.

A function constructs a mapping:

x\xrightarrow{f}y

Both establish rules inside a bounded space. Both can be internally consistent. Both can be instantiated. Both can produce consequences outside the representation.

But neither being constructed tells us whether its claims about external reality are true.

So:

\boxed{\text{Made up}\neq\text{meaningless}}

and

\boxed{\text{Functional}\neq\text{universally true}}

A fictional bridge can teach you about a real danger.

An invented mathematical function can describe a real trajectory.

An invented rule can reorganize real people.

A made-up story can alter a real decision.

Construction is itself one of the ways minds interface with reality.

